    {
      "id": "r221h42",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning of Sound"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Sound is a form of energy produced by vibrating bodies that travels as longitudinal mechanical waves.",
        "Sound requires a material medium to propagate and cannot travel through a vacuum.",
        "The pitch of a sound is determined by its wave frequency, while loudness depends on the wave amplitude.",
        "An echo is generated by the reflection of sound waves from an obstacle at a minimum distance of 17.2 meters.",
        "Ultrasound refers to sound frequencies above 20 kHz and is widely used in SONAR and medical diagnostics."
      ],
      "id": "dyhlfn4"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s5y2ewu",
      "content": "• Sound is a form of energy that produces the sensation of hearing."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ywdqocj",
      "content": "• It is produced by vibrating objects."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "q6iytqx",
      "content": "• Sound travels through a medium in the form of waves."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jdj45rf",
      "content": "• It cannot travel through vacuum because it requires a material medium."
    },
    {
      "id": "w8ixca9",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Production of Sound"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mww2xed",
      "content": "• Sound is produced when an object vibrates."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x30hvg1",
      "content": "• The vibrating object causes nearby particles of the medium to vibrate."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7gkanxj",
      "content": "• These vibrations travel through the medium as sound waves."
    },
    {
      "id": "ov2pn5t",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Sound Waves"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lprl9p9",
      "content": "Sound waves are longitudinal waves consisting of compressions and rarefactions."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bv8vtc9",
      "content": "• Compression – region of high pressure."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8w6h7d3",
      "content": "• Rarefaction – region of low pressure."
    },
    {
      "id": "dllp9xa",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Characteristics of Sound Waves"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Sound waves are mechanical longitudinal waves that require a material medium for their propagation.",
      "id": "bai97rm"
    },
    {
      "id": "l1f53n0",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Characteristic</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Explanation</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Amplitude</p>",
            "<p>Maximum displacement of particles; determines loudness</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Frequency</p>",
            "<p>Number of vibrations per second</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Wavelength</p>",
            "<p>Distance between two compressions</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Time Period</p>",
            "<p>Time taken for one complete vibration</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "8wwl8fr",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Types of Sound"
    },
    {
      "id": "iq0egie",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Frequency Range</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Infrasonic</p>",
            "<p>Less than 20 Hz</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Audible Sound</p>",
            "<p>20 Hz – 20,000 Hz</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Ultrasonic</p>",
            "<p>Greater than 20,000 Hz</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3pmh8x1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Speed of Sound"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "evc742b",
      "content": "The speed of sound depends on the medium and temperature. It travels fastest in solids and slowest in gases."
    },
    {
      "id": "nsq8h4k",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Medium</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Speed</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Air</p>",
            "<p>343 m/s</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Water</p>",
            "<p>1500 m/s</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Steel</p>",
            "<p>5000 m/s</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "l6taozb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Reflection of Sound"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r0ecdr4",
      "content": "When sound waves strike a surface, they bounce back. This phenomenon is called reflection of sound."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Parameter",
        "Infrasonic Waves",
        "Ultrasonic Waves"
      ],
      "rows": [
